WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.880
From the voice of a beloved children’s cartoon&nbsp;
to a star whose career didn’t begin until 50,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.320
these actors may be gone, but their mark&nbsp;
on the entertainment world is everlasting.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.940
French actor Gaspard Ulliel, who was&nbsp;
best known to international audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.320
for his turn as a young Hannibal Lecter,&nbsp;
died due to a skiing accident in January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.960
2022. He was 37. Ulliel began his career&nbsp;
on French television in the late 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.660
and was landing film roles by the early&nbsp;
2000s. His big break arrived in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:36.420
when he landed a role in "A Very Long Engagement,"&nbsp;
for which he won his first Cesar award. His second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.700
Cesar came in 2017, when he scooped up Best&nbsp;
Actor for "It's Only the End of the World."

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.200
Ulliel would make the transition to Hollywood&nbsp;
soon after, playing the titular killer in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.060
2007 prequel film "Hannibal Rising." It was&nbsp;
his biggest role at the time of his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.560
though that likely won't remain the case.&nbsp;
Sadly, Ulliel died a few months before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.180
Marvel's "Moon Knight" dropped, in which&nbsp;
he plays Anton Mogart, aka Midnight Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.734
a black market antiquities dealer who&nbsp;
goes head to head with the titular hero.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:08.520
Peter Robbins, who was best known as&nbsp;
the original voice of Charlie Brown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.400
died in January 2022. Dylan Novak, Robbins'&nbsp;
friend and agent, told the Los Angeles Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.560
that the cause of death was suicide. He was&nbsp;
65. The convention regular made his on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.660
debut in the 60s and appeared on TV shows&nbsp;
like "Rawhide" and "The Munsters." Robbins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:30.180
began voicing Charlie Brown in 1965 and went&nbsp;
on to play the classic "Peanuts" character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:35.100
seven times before the end of the decade,&nbsp;
most notably in the feature-length films "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.780
Charlie Brown Christmas," "It's the Great Pumpkin,&nbsp;
Charlie Brown," and "A Boy Named Charlie Brown."

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.120
According to the Times, Robbins was so proud of&nbsp;
his work on the franchise that he got a tattoo of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.520
Charlie Brown and Snoopy on his arm. Robbins was&nbsp;
also known for another comic adaptation: He played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.440
Alexander Bumstead on the live-action "Blondie"&nbsp;
series, based on the long-running strip of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.418
same name. Other notable credits include the TV&nbsp;
shows "F Troop," "Get Smart," and "My Three Sons."

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.820
Up-and-coming actor Moses J. Moseley, who&nbsp;
appeared on shows like "Queen of the South"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.080
and "The Walking Dead," was found dead&nbsp;
in Georgia in January 2022. His agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.240
Tabatha Minchew, told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.460
"Moses was a very talented person, with a&nbsp;
bright light around him. He will be missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:23.160
deeply by his friends, family and fans.&nbsp;
Always a ball of happy energy around him."

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.000
The cause of death was not immediately&nbsp;
revealed by authorities, who launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.300
an investigation to determine&nbsp;
the circumstances. He was 31.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.980
Moseley worked as a model before transitioning&nbsp;
into acting. He got his foot in the door with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.660
a number of uncredited appearances in films like&nbsp;
"Trouble with the Curve," "The Internship," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.580
"The Hunger Games: Catching Fire." Moseley began&nbsp;
playing Michonne's pet walker Mike on "The Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.340
Dead" in 2012, and would appear on the hit AMC&nbsp;
show half a dozen times over the next few years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.280
His most notable feature film was 2017's "Attack&nbsp;
of the Southern Fried Zombies." Moseley had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.840
several projects in various stages of production&nbsp;
when he was reported missing by his family.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:05.880
Robert Morse is probably best known&nbsp;
to contemporary audiences for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.820
role as old-school ad man and co-founder Bert&nbsp;
Cooper on "Mad Men." With its 1960s setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.420
business world plots, and one&nbsp;
very memorable musical number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:22.320
"Mad Men" easily brings Morse's first claim to&nbsp;
fame to mind: He won a Tony Award in 1962 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.640
his role as the accidentally successful&nbsp;
window washer-turned-corporate star J.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.900
Pierrepont Finch in the Broadway musical "How&nbsp;
to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying."

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.760
Morse appeared in many other stage productions&nbsp;
before and after that breakthrough hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.200
including "The Front Page" and "Tru," a&nbsp;
one-man play about writer Truman Capote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.420
which won Morse his second Tony Award — and&nbsp;
an Emmy for the TV adaptation. He was all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:51.300
over TV and film for decades as well, including&nbsp;
appearances on "Teen Titans Go!," "American Crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.280
Story," and the movie version of "How to Succeed&nbsp;
in Business Without Really Trying." On April 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:03.060
2022, Morse's son, Charlie, reported to KABC&nbsp;
that his father had died. Morse was 90 years old.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.940
Mike Hagerty was among the most prolific and&nbsp;
recognizable character actors of the last 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:14.400
years, popping up in more than 100 movies&nbsp;
and TV shows. He usually played a gruff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.140
blue-collar, working man type with a big&nbsp;
mustache and pronounced Chicagoan accent.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.520
"My mom was right. I'm just a big&nbsp;
potato with arms and legs and a head."

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.340
Among Hagerty's most familiar roles was Davey,&nbsp;
a cable TV station employee in "Wayne's World,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.480
apartment building superintendent Mr. Treeger&nbsp;
on "Friends," the best friend of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.680
character in the original "Overboard,"&nbsp;
an auto shop owner on "Lucky Louie," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.600
a main cast role on "The George Carlin Show."&nbsp;
Hagerty recently played a captain on multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.980
episodes of "Brooklyn Nine-Nine" and played&nbsp;
farmer Ed Miller on "Somebody Somewhere."

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.020
On Instagram, "Somebody Somewhere" star Bridget&nbsp;
Everett reported that Hagerty had died on May 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.720
2022, in Los Angeles. Cause of death wasn't&nbsp;
revealed for the actor, who was 67 years old.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.180
In a career spanning more than&nbsp;
200 roles back to the early 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.920
Philip Baker Hall became a quintessential&nbsp;
character actor, lending his raspy voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.260
and intense stare to countless movies and TV&nbsp;
shows. Hall became a favorite of filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:18.240
relatively late in his career. He's probably&nbsp;
best known to audiences as Lt. Joe Bookman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.540
a taciturn "library cop" tenaciously investigating&nbsp;
Jerry Seineld for a decades-overdue book on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.040
comedian's titular show. You'll also likely&nbsp;
recognize him as the intimidating but secretly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.360
sweet neighbor Walt on "Modern Family,"&nbsp;
Doctor Morrison on "Curb Your Enthusiasm,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.800
and hippo game show host Hank Hippopopalous&nbsp;
on "BoJack Horseman." Writer-director Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.120
Thomas Anderson also cast Hall in prominent&nbsp;
roles in "Hard Eight" and "Boogie Nights."

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.360
Hall's daughter shared that the actor&nbsp;
died at home in Glendale, California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.200
on June 12, 2022. Hall was 90 years old.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.960
While he had a long career as a character actor&nbsp;
for more than 40 years, Gregory Itzin was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.940
best known for his role on the smash-hit action&nbsp;
drama "24." Itzin portrayed Charles Logan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.440
a conniving vice president who finagles&nbsp;
his way into the presidency. The part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.940
earned Itzin two Emmy Award nominations,&nbsp;
one for Outstanding Supporting Actor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:13.260
a Drama Series and one for Guest Actor&nbsp;
when he returned late in the "24" run.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.900
In addition to roles on various "Star Trek" shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:21.900
"The Mentalist," "NCIS," "Covert Affairs," and&nbsp;
"Friends," Itzin performed a bit part in 1980's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.700
"Airplane!" as an airport-working cult recruiter.&nbsp;
Tragically, Itzin underwent an emergency surgery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.040
in July 2022, but complications from&nbsp;
the procedure proved fatal. He was 74.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.500
Perhaps the quintessential supporting&nbsp;
actor specializing in playing elegant but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.060
intimidating mobsters, Tony Sirico found&nbsp;
the role of his life as Paulie Walnuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.446
on HBO's universally acclaimed&nbsp;
modern-day Mafia saga "The Sopranos."

00:06:46.446 --> 00:06:48.726
"You do what you're told. When you’re told."

00:06:48.726 --> 00:06:53.220
He played similar characters in "Goodfellas,"&nbsp;
"Bullets Over Broadway," and "Mighty Aphrodite."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:58.320
His first film work came as an extra in the&nbsp;
organized crime drama "Crazy Joe." Sirico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.520
turned to playing fictional law-breakers&nbsp;
in order to escape a real life of actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.740
crime. He was arrested 28 times as a kid and&nbsp;
young adult, and he had connections to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.240
Colombo crime family. In recent years,&nbsp;
and after "The Sopranos," Sirico traded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.980
on his image and tendency to be typecast,&nbsp;
playing tough characters on comedy shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.760
like a gangster on "American Dad"&nbsp;
and a gruff dog on "Family Guy."

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:27.240
Sirico's manager confirmed to media outlets&nbsp;
that the actor died July 8, 2022. He was 79.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.820
Paul Sorvino worked in character roles&nbsp;
and on stage throughout the '60s and '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.660
earning a Tony nomination for "That&nbsp;
Championship Season" on Broadway in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.860
1973. Following a starring role on the&nbsp;
short-lived "The Streets of San Francisco"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:46.200
spinoff "Bert D'Angelo/Superstar," Sorvino&nbsp;
reprised his role as Phil Romano for the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.520
version of "That Championship Season."&nbsp;
It was his screen acting breakthrough.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.680
In 1990, Sorvino landed roles in major&nbsp;
movies that would amount to his signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.900
work — gangster Lips Manlis in the big-screen&nbsp;
adaptation of "Dick Tracy," and as the paternal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:05.700
calculating mob boss Paulie Cicero in the crime&nbsp;
saga "Goodfellas." In addition to criminals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.960
Sorvino played a lot of authority figures,&nbsp;
like a sergeant early in the run of "Law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.620
and Order," Juliet's father in Baz&nbsp;
Luhrmann's "Romeo + Juliet," Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.240
Kissinger in Oliver Stone's "Nixon," and&nbsp;
Frank Costello in "Godfather of Harlem."

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.500
Sorvino died on July 25, 2022.&nbsp;
According to the actor's publicist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:25.920
natural causes were to blame. Sorvino was 83.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.620
Tony Dow is most remembered for a signature&nbsp;
role that came early in his life and career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:39.840
From 1957 to 1963, he played squeaky-clean 1950s&nbsp;
teenager Wally Cleaver on "Leave It to Beaver," a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.300
hit show of the golden age of TV. After "Beaver"&nbsp;
wrapped up, Dow played Chet on the melodramatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.800
teen-oriented soap opera "Never Too Young," but&nbsp;
he would only act occasionally in the decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:54.120
thereafter. Dow guest-starred on shows&nbsp;
like "Adam-12" and "Murder She Wrote."

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.560
He frequently returned to the role that made&nbsp;
him famous, whether it was co-starring on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.760
1980s reboot "The New Leave It to Beaver"&nbsp;
or sending up his character in a "Kentucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.380
Fried Movie" cameo. Dow segued into a&nbsp;
career as TV show director in the '80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.040
ultimately helming episodes of&nbsp;
shows like "Coach" and "Babylon 5."

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:18.240
Dow received a cancer diagnosis in May 2022.&nbsp;
In July 2022, the actor died at the age of 77.

00:09:18.395 --> 00:09:24.480
Leslie Phillips had roles in more than 170&nbsp;
projects, in a career that lasted for well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.480
over 80 years. And yet he's best known — to&nbsp;
younger, American audiences, at least — for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.160
role in which he didn't appear on-screen at&nbsp;
all: He voiced the mischievous Sorting Hat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.600
in "Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone" and&nbsp;
"Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2."

00:09:39.747 --> 00:09:43.707
"Mmm, difficult, very difficult."

00:09:43.707 --> 00:09:47.640
Other major career-topping performances&nbsp;
came in the drama "Venus," for which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:53.820
was nominated for a BAFTA and other awards in&nbsp;
2007, and in 2001's "Lara Croft: Tomb Raider."

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.240
One of the most prolific and&nbsp;
quintessentially British actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.740
Phillips often played British archetypes,&nbsp;
appearing in made-for-TV stage adaptations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.240
and in multiple roles in the long-running&nbsp;
"Carry On" franchise in the mid-20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.120
which lampooned British life and sensibilities.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:16.560
Phillips died in his sleep on November 7, 2022,&nbsp;
after a lengthy illness. The actor was 98.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:22.020
Clarence Gilyard Jr. found success in both acting&nbsp;
and academia. On the educational side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.260
he earned his bachelor's degree in&nbsp;
acting at California State University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.940
Long Beach and California State University,&nbsp;
Dominguez Hills. Later on, he would earn an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.760
MFA in Theatre Performance at&nbsp;
Southern Methodist University.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.920
On the entertainment side, Gilyard made his&nbsp;
cinematic debut in 1986's "Top Gun," playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.300
the pilot with the call sign "Sundown." He&nbsp;
would then join one of the most memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:48.300
bad guy crews in action history, teaming up&nbsp;
with Alan Rickman's Hans Gruber in "Die Hard."

00:10:48.434 --> 00:10:51.000
"Theo!" "Yo!"

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.940
But he truly endeared himself to TV fans in&nbsp;
"Walker, Texas Ranger" as Chuck Norris' sidekick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:03.180
Jimmy Trivette. Sadly, Gilyard died in November&nbsp;
2022 at the age of 66, with the University of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.500
Nevada, Las Vegas confirming his death and film&nbsp;
chair Heather Addison saying, via Deadline,

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:13.238
"Professor Gilyard was a beacon of light and&nbsp;
strength for everyone around him at UNLV."

00:11:13.534 --> 00:11:18.540
Helen Slayton-Hughes was best known for playing&nbsp;
the incredibly cranky Ethel Beavers on "Parks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:23.220
Recreation." Ethel was a court stenographer&nbsp;
who was immune to Tom Haverford's charms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.480
was adopted by Andy and April to be their&nbsp;
grandmother. Of course, that's far from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:33.060
only role she played. Born in 1930, Slayton-Hughes&nbsp;
didn't begin acting until age 50. Over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:37.620
she's popped up in television shows such as&nbsp;
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine," "New Girl," "Arrested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:41.160
Development," "True Blood," and "The&nbsp;
West Wing." She also appeared in movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.360
like the Oscar-nominated "Good Night, and&nbsp;
Good Luck" and the "Veronica Mars" movie.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.260
Slayton-Hughes' death was confirmed by&nbsp;
her family on Facebook on December 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:51.240
2022, with a statement that read,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.180
"Helen passed away last night. Her pain has ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:58.800
but her fierce spirit lives on. Thank you&nbsp;
for the love and support of her work."

